@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Date & Time Functions";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            SQL Server Date &amp; Time Functions
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            Date and Time Functions in SQL Server are used to work
            with dates, times, extract date parts, calculate date
            differences and manipulate date values.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are Date &amp; Time Functions?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Date and Time Functions are built-in SQL Server functions
            used to retrieve, manipulate and calculate date and time
            values.
        </p>

        <p>
            These functions are commonly used in applications for
            storing registration dates, joining dates, order dates,
            payment dates, birthdays, deadlines and timestamps.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">

            <strong>Example:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                Suppose an Employee table contains an employee's
                joining date. Date functions can be used to find
                the year of joining, calculate the employee's
                experience or find the number of days between
                two dates.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         COMMON DATE FUNCTIONS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common Date &amp; Time Functions
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>GETDATE()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the current system date and time.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>GETDATE()</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>SYSDATETIME()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the current system date and time
                            with higher precision.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>SYSDATETIME()</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>GETUTCDATE()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the current UTC date and time.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>GETUTCDATE()</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DATEPART()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns a specific part of a date.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>DATEPART(YEAR, GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>YEAR()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the year from a date.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>YEAR(GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>MONTH()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the month from a date.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>MONTH(GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DAY()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the day from a date.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>DAY(GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DATEADD()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Adds a specified time interval to a date.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>DATEADD(DAY, 10, GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DATEDIFF()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Calculates the difference between two dates.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>DATEDIFF(DAY, '2025-01-01', GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>EOMONTH()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the last day of a month.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>EOMONTH(GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         1. GETDATE()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. GETDATE() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>GETDATE()</strong> function returns the
            current system date and time of the SQL Server.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>GETDATE()</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT GETDATE();</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

            <strong>Output:</strong>

            <br />

            The current date and time of the SQL Server.

        </div>

        <p>
            The exact output depends on the system date and time
            when the query is executed.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         2. SYSDATETIME()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. SYSDATETIME() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>SYSDATETIME()</strong> function returns the
            current system date and time with higher fractional
            seconds precision than GETDATE().
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SYSDATETIME()</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT SYSDATETIME();</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         3. GETUTCDATE()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. GETUTCDATE() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>GETUTCDATE()</strong> function returns the
            current UTC date and time.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>GETUTCDATE()</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT GETUTCDATE();</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
            UTC stands for <strong>Coordinated Universal Time</strong>.
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         4. DATEPART()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            4. DATEPART() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DATEPART()</strong> function returns a
            specific part of a date such as year, month, day,
            hour or minute.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>DATEPART(datepart, date)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DATEPART(YEAR, GETDATE());</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> Current year
        </div>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            More Examples
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DATEPART(MONTH, GETDATE());

SELECT DATEPART(DAY, GETDATE());

SELECT DATEPART(HOUR, GETDATE());</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         5. YEAR()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            5. YEAR() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>YEAR()</strong> function extracts the year
            from a date value.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>YEAR(date)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT YEAR('2026-10-05');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 2026
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         6. MONTH()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            6. MONTH() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>MONTH()</strong> function returns the month
            number from a date.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>MONTH(date)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT MONTH('2026-10-05');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 10
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         7. DAY()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            7. DAY() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DAY()</strong> function returns the day
            number from a date.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>DAY(date)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DAY('2026-10-05');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 5
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         8. DATEADD()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            8. DATEADD() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DATEADD()</strong> function adds a specified
            number of date or time units to a date.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>DATEADD(datepart, number, date)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DATEADD(DAY, 10, '2026-10-05');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 2026-10-15
        </div>

        <p>
            Here, 10 days are added to the given date.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            More Examples
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DATEADD(MONTH, 2, '2026-10-05');

SELECT DATEADD(YEAR, 1, '2026-10-05');

SELECT DATEADD(DAY, -5, '2026-10-05');</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         9. DATEDIFF()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            9. DATEDIFF() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>DATEDIFF()</strong> function returns the
            difference between two dates based on the specified
            date part.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>DATEDIFF(datepart, startdate, enddate)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DATEDIFF(DAY, '2026-10-01', '2026-10-05');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 4
        </div>

        <p>
            There are four days between October 1 and October 5.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Calculate Years
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT DATEDIFF(YEAR, '2020-01-01', '2026-01-01');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 6
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         10. EOMONTH()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            10. EOMONTH() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>EOMONTH()</strong> function returns the last
            day of the month for a specified date.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>EOMONTH(start_date)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT EOMONTH('2026-10-05');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 2026-10-31
        </div>

        <p>
            October has 31 days, so the last day of the month is
            October 31.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL TABLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example with Employee Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we have an Employees table:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Employees
(
    EmployeeId INT,
    EmployeeName VARCHAR(100),
    JoiningDate DATE
);</code></pre>


        <p class="mt-4">
            Insert sample records:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Employees
VALUES
(101, 'Rahul Sharma', '2020-01-15'),
(102, 'Priya Patil', '2022-06-10'),
(103, 'Amit Kumar', '2024-03-20');</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Find Joining Year
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    JoiningDate,
    YEAR(JoiningDate) AS JoiningYear
FROM Employees;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Find Joining Month
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    MONTH(JoiningDate) AS JoiningMonth
FROM Employees;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Find Number of Days Since Joining
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    DATEDIFF(DAY, JoiningDate, GETDATE()) AS DaysCompleted
FROM Employees;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Add 1 Year to Joining Date
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    DATEADD(YEAR, 1, JoiningDate) AS NextYearDate
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         DATEADD VS DATEDIFF
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DATEADD() vs DATEDIFF()
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>DATEADD()</td>

                        <td>
                            Adds or subtracts a date interval.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>DATEADD(DAY, 10, GETDATE())</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>DATEDIFF()</td>

                        <td>
                            Finds the difference between two dates.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>DATEDIFF(DAY, StartDate, EndDate)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHEN TO USE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            When Do We Use Date &amp; Time Functions?
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To get the current date and time.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To extract year, month and day from a date.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To calculate the difference between two dates.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To add or subtract days, months or years.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To calculate employee experience.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                To calculate order and delivery dates.
            </li>

            <li>
                To find the beginning or end of a date period.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning">

            <h4 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    GETDATE() returns the current SQL Server date and time.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    YEAR(), MONTH() and DAY() extract individual
                    date components.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DATEPART() can extract different parts of a date.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DATEADD() adds or subtracts a date interval.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DATEDIFF() calculates the difference between dates.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    EOMONTH() returns the last day of a month.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Date functions are widely used in real-world
                    database applications.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                SQL Server Date and Time Functions are used to work
                with date and time values. In this topic, we learned
                GETDATE(), SYSDATETIME(), GETUTCDATE(), DATEPART(),
                YEAR(), MONTH(), DAY(), DATEADD(), DATEDIFF() and
                EOMONTH(). These functions are extremely useful for
                employee records, orders, payments, reports,
                scheduling and date-based calculations.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


